From d841f3c95361a9b8ffc2d3eeea4be9df9b4b0722 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fit a stereo figure to the person's depth limits

A stereo figure's 3D axes are drawn for each eye with the separation
fitted to the depth limits the desktop gives the figure's window (Sparky
Stereo OS's KWin, read through libstereo-declare), in place of the
figure property stereoeyeseparation:

- Before the left eye the figure is drawn once from the camera between
  the eyes into the left buffer.  At the end of each 3D axes (Octave
  clears the depth buffer at the start of every axes) the renderer
  reads its depth buffer and keeps, by the axes' handle, the nearest
  and farthest distance of its pixels, the distance of its camera
  target and the scale from camera units to buffer pixels.

- Each eye shears the axes' orthographic projection about the camera
  target, as before; a point at the distance d stands
  t * xscale * (d - target) pixels further right in the right eye.  The
  separation t of each axes is the largest under which its nearest
  pixel stays inside the popped limit and its farthest inside the sunk
  limit.  A 2D axes is neither sheared nor measured.

- The limits are read four times a second, on Wayland for the
  top-level window's surface and on X11 on the drawing area's own
  window, in the pixels of the figure's buffer; a change redraws the
  figure.  When they are not offered, do not arrive within two seconds
  or cannot be read, when the depth cannot be read, or when a figure
  that asked for stereo buffers gets none on a desktop that offers
  them, the figure asks the interpreter to tell the person once
  (sparky_stereo_tell, an m-file of the edition with the sentences in
  ten languages).  A script that sets stereoeyeseparation is told once
  that it no longer decides the depth.

- Only the drawing area asks for stereo buffers (its own format): the
  figure window is no longer created with a stereo default format, so
  menus, toolbar and controls stay one picture.

- A Qt figure's Tools menu gets the edition's Enable 3D and its 3D
  display settings (sparky_stereo_menu, when the edition's m-file is
  on the path).

Print and getframe draw one view, from the camera between the eyes, as
before.

